# No deficiency

Heritage Federal Bank is filing this SAR on customer Nicole Parker, age 41, of Atlanta, Georgia, regarding suspected funnel-account activity and possible laundering of third-party funds through personal checking account 72041853. Parker opened the account on May 6, 2024. At account opening, she identified her occupation as a registered nurse employed by Piedmont Regional Medical Center, reported annual income of approximately $84,000, and stated that the account would be used for payroll deposits, household expenses, and routine personal transactions.

From June 9, 2026, through August 3, 2026, Parker’s account received 10 cash deposits totaling $117,450. The deposits ranged from $8,900 to $15,200 and were made at Heritage Federal Bank branches in Georgia, Florida, Tennessee, and South Carolina.

The deposits were as follows:

June 9: $11,800 deposited in Jacksonville, Florida.
June 14: $9,450 deposited in Savannah, Georgia.
June 21: $12,600 deposited in Nashville, Tennessee.
June 29: $8,900 deposited in Charleston, South Carolina.
July 5: $15,200 deposited in Jacksonville, Florida.
July 11: $10,750 deposited in Macon, Georgia.
July 18: $13,400 deposited in Knoxville, Tennessee.
July 23: $9,600 deposited in Savannah, Georgia.
July 29: $14,250 deposited in Charleston, South Carolina.
August 3: $11,500 deposited in Jacksonville, Florida.

Branch records and available surveillance showed that multiple individuals conducted the deposits. Parker was not observed making any of the deposits outside Georgia. Identification information was collected for four depositors where required by branch procedures. The identified individuals were not joint owners or authorized signers on Parker’s account, and Heritage Federal Bank identified no apparent family or employment relationship between Parker and those individuals through available customer information and public-record research.

Between June 10 and August 5, 2026, Parker conducted five large cash withdrawals totaling $110,800 at Heritage Federal Bank branches in the Atlanta metropolitan area. The withdrawals were $24,500 on June 10, $18,600 on June 23, $22,750 on July 7, $19,400 on July 20, and $25,550 on August 5.

Each withdrawal occurred within two business days of one or more of the out-of-area cash deposits. Parker conducted all five withdrawals in person. Branch personnel completed applicable Currency Transaction Reports for the withdrawals.

Historical review of the account from May 2024 through May 2026 showed primarily biweekly payroll deposits ranging from approximately $2,700 to $3,100, along with mortgage payments, utilities, credit card payments, retail purchases, insurance expenses, and routine personal transfers. Prior to June 2026, Parker had received fewer than $4,000 in total cash deposits during the entire account relationship and had not conducted a cash withdrawal exceeding $3,500.

On July 7, 2026, a branch employee asked Parker about the source and purpose of the recent cash activity while processing her $22,750 withdrawal. Parker stated that several friends and acquaintances were pooling money for informal real-estate investments and that she was helping collect and distribute the funds because she had “the easiest bank access.”

Parker stated that the withdrawn funds were being provided to an individual she identified as “Marcus” who was coordinating property purchases. Parker did not provide Marcus’s full name, company name, address, telephone number, or other identifying information.

On July 15, 2026, Heritage Federal Bank contacted Parker through its enhanced review process and requested additional information concerning the investment arrangement. Parker stated that the group had not formed a legal entity and that she did not maintain written agreements with the individuals providing funds. She did not provide property addresses, purchase contracts, closing documents, investment agreements, receipts, or other records showing that the deposited or withdrawn funds were connected to real-estate transactions.

Parker stated that she did not receive compensation for handling the funds.

Open-source and public-record review identified no business entity registered to Parker associated with real estate, investment management, money transmission, or financial services. The bank identified no real-estate purchases, mortgage payments, title-company transactions, attorney trust-account payments, or other account activity consistent with the stated investment purpose during the review period.

Heritage Federal Bank considers the activity suspicious because Parker’s personal account received substantial cash deposits conducted by multiple third parties across four states, followed shortly by large cash withdrawals by Parker in Georgia. The activity represents a significant deviation from Parker’s historical account use and stated customer profile.

The geographic separation between the individuals depositing cash and Parker withdrawing the funds, combined with Parker’s limited explanation of the purpose of the transactions and inability to provide documentation supporting the stated investment activity, is consistent with possible funnel-account activity and movement of funds on behalf of unidentified third parties. Heritage Federal Bank has not determined the source of the deposited cash, the ultimate recipient of the withdrawn funds, or whether Parker knowingly participated in criminal activity.

Heritage Federal Bank placed the account under enhanced monitoring on August 6, 2026. The account remains open. Account statements, deposit and withdrawal records, applicable Currency Transaction Reports, branch surveillance, depositor identification information where available, customer communications, customer due diligence records, and public-record research are available upon request.
